      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-Region Relative Entropy",
        "statement": "Assume AC. If $P$ and $Q$ are probability measures on the same measurable space, define\n$$D(P\\Vert Q)=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\int h\\log h\\,dQ,&P\\ll Q\\text{ and }h=dP/dQ,\\\\+\\infty,&P\\not\\ll Q.\\end{cases}$$\nUse $0\\log0=0$. The Radon–Nikodym derivative $h$ is unique up to $Q$-almost-everywhere equality, so the integral is independent of its representative. Its negative part is finite because $-u\\log u\\leq1/e$ for $0\\leq u\\leq1$ and $Q$ is a probability; hence $D(P\\Vert Q)$ is a well-defined extended real number.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "thm-radon-nikodym-density-exists-and-is-unique-up-to-almost-everywhere-equality",
          "def-probability-measure",
          "def-natural-logarithm"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-probability-measure": "mathematical-premise",
