      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Local-Tame Topology",
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$ be the probability laws $P$ on the configuration space of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]] that are invariant under every $T_x$ and satisfy $E_PN_{C_0}<\\infty$. Stationarity then makes every bounded-region count integrable, because a bounded set meets finitely many unit cells.\n\nA real measurable function $f$ on $\\Omega$ is local if there is a bounded Borel set $A$ such that $f$ is measurable with respect to the restriction $\\omega\\mapsto\\omega|_A$. It is tame if there is a finite $b\\geq0$ such that $|f(\\omega)|\\leq b(1+N_A(\\omega))$ for every $\\omega$ and one such bounded $A$. Write $\\mathcal T$ for the family of all local tame functions. Each $f\\in\\mathcal T$ is integrable under every $P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$.\n\nThe local-tame topology $\\tau_L$ on $\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$ is the initial topology generated by all maps $P\\mapsto E_P f$, $f\\in\\mathcal T$. Equivalently, a net $(P_\\alpha)$ converges to $P$ in $\\tau_L$ exactly when $E_{P_\\alpha}f\\to E_Pf$ for every measurable local $f$ with at most linear growth in the count of a bounded support. Bounded local indicators are among these tests, so this topology separates distinct probability laws. The domain restriction to finite-intensity stationary laws ensures every displayed expectation is a finite real number.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
