      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-spatial-mean-information",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Spatial Mean-Information Theorem",
        "statement": "Assume AC. Let $d\\geq1$, let $P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$, with this class defined in [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]], be ergodic under the full translation group $\\mathbb R^d$, and suppose its specific relative entropy $I(P)=\\gamma$ relative to the unit-intensity Poisson law $Q$ is finite. Ergodic means that every measurable event invariant modulo $P$-null sets under each translation has probability $0$ or $1$. For an integer $N\\geq1$, let $\\Lambda_N=[0,N)^d$, and let $h_N=dP_{\\Lambda_N}/dQ_{\\Lambda_N}$, which exists because $D(P_{\\Lambda_N}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_N})<\\infty$. Then\n$$\\frac{1}{N^d}\\log h_N\\longrightarrow\\gamma\\qquad\\text{in }L^1(P).$$\nWithout the full-translation ergodicity hypothesis the proof yields an invariant $L^1(P)$ limit of mean $\\gamma$; it need not be constant.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference",
          "thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties",
          "def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy",
